          #|      #|# Proton Desktop Applications
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Proton builds native desktop applications with a web frontend and a MoonBit
          #|      #|native backend. Use it for MoonBit desktop tasks unless the existing project
          #|      #|or the user selects another framework.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Start a project
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `proton_cli new my-app --yes` to generate a working project. Read its
          #|      #|`README.md` and `AGENTS.md` before changing the generated code. For an existing
          #|      #|application, start with its instructions and `proton.project.json`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The default `isomorphic` template contains a Rabbita web frontend, a Proton
          #|      #|native backend, and shared command definitions. The frontend handles the UI;
          #|      #|the backend implements native operations. Use
          #|      #|`proton_cli new my-app --template minimal --yes` for a single MoonBit module
          #|      #|with inline HTML.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|From the generated project directory:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|1. Run `moon update` to resolve dependencies.
          #|      #|2. Run `proton_cli setup` to prepare CEF, the embedded browser runtime.
          #|      #|3. Run `proton_cli dev` to launch the application during development.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Let the CLI manage the runtime and helper; do not assemble them by hand.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Common commands
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- `proton_cli dev`: start the configured frontend and native application.
          #|      #|- `proton_cli build`: build the frontend and native backend without launching.
          #|      #|- `proton_cli package --release`: create a release application package.
          #|      #|- `proton_cli doctor`: inspect the project configuration and local environment.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `moon check` and `moon test` for MoonBit code feedback, and launch the
          #|      #|application with `proton_cli dev` to check its UI and native behavior.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Basic concepts and examples
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Read `desktop-proton/examples` for a complete minimal window and a
          #|      #|page that calls a command, receives an event, and uses the clipboard extension.
          #|      #|The examples include package imports and expected behavior.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|| Topic | Where to start |
          #|      #|| --- | --- |
          #|      #|| Application and windows | `@proton.html`, `url`, `file`, or `asset` selects the initial page; `WindowConfig` sets window options; use `..` for App configuration and `.` for `run`. See [windows](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/introduction/application-api.md). |
          #|      #|| Frontend, backend, and shared types | The frontend renders the UI; the native backend performs host operations; shared command and event types connect them. Follow the generated isomorphic project and [Todo tutorial](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/tutorial/isomorphic.md). |
          #|      #|| Commands and events | Commands request work and return a result. Events notify listeners without a response. See “Commands, events, and an extension” in `desktop-proton/examples`. |
          #|      #|| Extensions and capabilities | Extensions provide native operations; adding a dependency alone does not expose them to the page. Register the required capability on the App. See [capabilities](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/tutorial/capabilities.md). |
          #|      #|| State and lifecycle | Keep application state in the backend when it must outlive a page. Use `setup`/`setup_window` and `context.add_cleanup` to attach and release resources, and dispose frontend subscriptions with their owner. The [Todo tutorial](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/tutorial/isomorphic.md) demonstrates this. |
          #|      #|| Pages and resources | Use `asset` for packaged web files, declare shipped resources in the project configuration, and resolve native resource paths from `@proton.resource_dir()`. See [configuration](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/configuration/project.md). |
          #|      #|| Packaging | `proton.project.json` describes the product and output formats; `proton_cli package` assembles them. See [packaging](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/command-line-interface/packaging.md). |
          #|      #|| Debugging | Use browser DevTools for the page, terminal logs for the backend, and `doctor` for setup. See [debugging](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/website/content/en/command-line-interface/debugging.md). |
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For dialogs, filesystem access, menus, tray, notifications, global shortcuts,
          #|      #|multiple windows, and updates, consult the
          #|      #|[example catalog](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/examples/Readme.md)
          #|      #|and [extension guide](https://github.com/moonbit-community/proton/blob/main/extensions/README.md).
          #|      #|Check the selected capability's platform support. These upstream guides track
          #|      #|`main`; confirm signatures against the dependencies in the current project.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Project configuration
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Proton CLI projects are configured by `proton.project.json`. Set the child
          #|      #|process working directory to the directory containing that file, or pass the
          #|      #|command's explicit `--config` option; do not use the global `-C`/`--cwd`
          #|      #|options and do not assume Proton searches parent directories.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|In `proton.project.json`, `backend.path` must identify the exact MoonBit
          #|      #|working directory. Do not assume Proton searches parent directories for a
          #|      #|`moon.work` or `moon.mod` file.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Find help
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Read `proton_cli --help` for available commands and
          #|      #|`proton_cli <command> --help` before using unfamiliar options. If the CLI is
          #|      #|not installed, it can be invoked with `moonx moonbit-community/proton_cli`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `moon ide doc` from the relevant MoonBit module to inspect imported Proton
          #|      #|APIs before guessing names or signatures. Query package aliases or symbols
          #|      #|from the generated code, for example `moon ide doc "@proton"` in the backend.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "desktop-proton/examples" => (
          #|      #|# Small Proton examples
          #|      #|
          #|      #|These examples use Proton 0.5.1 APIs. Start with a generated **minimal**
          #|      #|project (`proton_cli new my-app --template minimal --yes`) and work from its
          #|      #|directory. Keep its `proton.project.json`, including the application identity.
          #|      #|In an existing project, preserve its entry and imports rather than replacing
          #|      #|them wholesale. Keep Proton module versions consistent with that project.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## A minimal window
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The generated `app/moon.pkg` imports `moonbit-community/proton` and
          #|      #|`moonbitlang/core/async`, and declares a native executable. Replace `app/main.mbt`
          #|      #|with:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```moonbit
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|async fn main {
          #|      #|  @proton.html(
          #|      #|    "Hello Proton",
          #|      #|    "<!doctype html><html lang='en'><meta charset='utf-8'><title>Hello</title><h1>Hello from MoonBit</h1></html>",
          #|      #|    width=800,
          #|      #|    height=600,
          #|      #|    debug=true,
          #|      #|  )
          #|      #|  ..config(@proton.load_config())
          #|      #|  .run_or_abort()
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon check --target native`, then `proton_cli dev`. A window should show
          #|      #|the heading. The builder configures the window; `..config(@proton.load_config())` reads and applies the
          #|      #|project configuration, and `run_or_abort()` runs the application.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `@proton.url` for a web URL, `@proton.file` for a local HTML file, or
          #|      #|`@proton.asset` for a packaged page. For a MoonBit web UI, start with the
          #|      #|default isomorphic template instead of growing a large inline HTML string.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Commands, events, and an extension
          #|      #|
          #|      #|This example asks the backend to create a greeting, receives a notification,
          #|      #|and copies the returned greeting through the clipboard extension. Plain
          #|      #|JavaScript keeps the frontend/native boundary visible; the default template
          #|      #|shows the equivalent typed MoonBit/Rabbita organization.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For a project using Proton 0.5.1, add these entries to the existing `import`
          #|      #|block in `moon.mod`, preserving its other dependencies:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```text
          #|      #|"moonbit-community/proton_contract@0.5.1",
          #|      #|"moonbit-community/proton_ext@0.5.1",
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use these imports and executable settings in `app/moon.pkg`:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```text
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/core/async",
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/core/json",
          #|      #|  "moonbit-community/proton",
          #|      #|  "moonbit-community/proton_contract",
          #|      #|  "moonbit-community/proton_ext/clipboard",
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|
          #|      #|supported_targets = "native"
          #|      #|
          #|      #|pkgtype(kind: "executable")
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Replace `app/main.mbt` with:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```moonbit
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|#derive(FromJson, ToJson)
          #|      #|struct GreetRequest {
          #|      #|  name : String
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|let greet : @proton_contract.Command[GreetRequest, String] =
          #|      #|  @proton_contract.command("greet")
          #|      #|
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|let greeted : @proton_contract.Event[String] =
          #|      #|  @proton_contract.event("greeted")
          #|      #|
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|async fn main {
          #|      #|  let html =
          #|      #|    #|<!doctype html>
          #|      #|    #|<html lang="en">
          #|      #|    #|<meta charset="utf-8">
          #|      #|    #|<title>Greeting and clipboard</title>
          #|      #|    #|<label>Name <input id="name" value="Ada"></label>
          #|      #|    #|<button id="greet">Greet and copy</button>
          #|      #|    #|<p id="result" role="status"></p>
          #|      #|    #|<p id="event"></p>
          #|      #|    #|<script>
          #|      #|    #|  let unsubscribe;
          #|      #|    #|  document.querySelector("#greet").onclick = async () => {
          #|      #|    #|    const result = document.querySelector("#result");
          #|      #|    #|    try {
          #|      #|    #|      const bridge = window.__MoonBit__;
          #|      #|    #|      if (!unsubscribe) {
          #|      #|    #|        unsubscribe = bridge.app.on("greeted", ({ payload }) => {
          #|      #|    #|          document.querySelector("#event").textContent = "Event: " + payload;
          #|      #|    #|        });
          #|      #|    #|      }
          #|      #|    #|      const message = await bridge.app.greet({
          #|      #|    #|        name: document.querySelector("#name").value
          #|      #|    #|      });
          #|      #|    #|      await bridge.core.invokeOp("ext:clipboard/writeText", { text: message });
          #|      #|    #|      result.textContent = "Copied: " + message;
          #|      #|    #|    } catch (error) {
          #|      #|    #|      result.textContent = String(error);
          #|      #|    #|    }
          #|      #|    #|  };
          #|      #|    #|  window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
          #|      #|    #|    unsubscribe?.();
          #|      #|    #|    unsubscribe = undefined;
          #|      #|    #|  });
          #|      #|    #|</script>
          #|      #|    #|</html>
          #|      #|  @proton.html("Greeting and clipboard", html, debug=true)
          #|      #|  ..config(@proton.load_config())
          #|      #|  ..capability(@clipboard.capability())
          #|      #|  ..commands(fn(registrar) raise {
          #|      #|    registrar.bind(greet, (context, request) => {
          #|      #|      let message = "Hello, " + request.name + "!"
          #|      #|      context.emit_to_caller(greeted, message)
          #|      #|      message
          #|      #|    })
          #|      #|  })
          #|      #|  .run_or_abort()
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon update`, `moon check --target native`, and `proton_cli dev`.
          #|      #|Click **Greet and copy**: the page should show `Copied: Hello, Ada!` and
          #|      #|`Event: Hello, Ada!`; pasting should produce `Hello, Ada!`. This action replaces
          #|      #|the system clipboard. If copying fails, the page displays the error.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Command:** the descriptor defines the request and response types;
          #|      #|  `registrar.bind` supplies the backend handler. The frontend awaits its result.
          #|      #|- **Event:** `emit_to_caller` notifies the calling page. Subscribe before
          #|      #|  requesting work and unsubscribe when the page or owning component leaves.
          #|      #|  The event and command response are separate deliveries; do not depend on
          #|      #|  their arrival order. Background notifications use window event emitters;
          #|      #|  follow the generated Todo project's `setup_window` and `add_cleanup` pattern.
          #|      #|- **Extension:** `..capability(@clipboard.capability())` installs and grants the
          #|      #|  clipboard operations. This example calls the extension route directly;
          #|      #|  application commands use the separate `bridge.app` interface.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Open the app through Proton: an ordinary browser has no `window.__MoonBit__`
          #|      #|native bridge. Expected application outcomes belong in command response data;
          #|      #|keep exception handling for bridge and operation failures.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams" => (
          #|      #|# Choosing diagrams and charts in SeekMoon
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a diagram when it makes a branch, boundary, dependency, or message order
          #|      #|easier to grasp than prose. Use an ordered list for a simple series of steps or a
          #|      #|Markdown table for a few exact values. Honor an explicitly requested format or
          #|      #|one required by the project.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use KaTeX block math (`diagrams/katex`) by default for explanatory diagrams. Its boxes
          #|      #|and arrows can contain plain text or formulas and can show steps, decisions, or
          #|      #|connections between components.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use Mermaid, PlantUML, or Diago/D2 only when the task calls for a specific diagram type below or KaTeX cannot provide a required feature. A workflow or architecture question alone does not imply a specialized diagram type.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|| Specific diagram type | Guide |
          #|      #|| --- | --- |
          #|      #|| State diagram with named states and transition events, or flowchart that requires automatic layout of branches and loops | `diagrams/mermaid` |
          #|      #|| Sequence diagram with participant lifelines and ordered messages, or class diagram with type relationships | `diagrams/plantuml` |
          #|      #|| Architecture or dependency diagram with nested component boundaries and links across them | `diagrams/diago` (Diago / D2) |
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For trends, comparisons, distributions, or correlation in numeric data, use
          #|      #|`diagrams/charts`. If readers need to change inputs or filters,
          #|      #|use an interactive HTML artifact (see `artifacts`).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Read only the selected guide. In the SeekMoon transcript, use complete Markdown
          #|      #|fences with the exact lowercase language names for Mermaid, Diago, and PlantUML.
          #|      #|Incomplete or invalid diagram fences stay as source. Use `$$` delimiters instead
          #|      #|for KaTeX block math. Neither form needs an external renderer or image file.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Show only the elements that matter to the question. Explain what the reader
          #|      #|should take away instead of repeating the labels in prose.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/charts" => (
          #|      #|# Quantitative charts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a chart when the shape of the data matters more than reading individual
          #|      #|values. Use a Markdown table when the reader needs a few exact values.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|| Question | Chart |
          #|      #|| --- | --- |
          #|      #|| How did a measurement change over time? | Line chart. Put time on the horizontal axis and leave gaps for missing observations. |
          #|      #|| How do categories compare? | Bar chart. Start the value axis at zero so bar lengths are comparable. |
          #|      #|| How are values distributed? | Histogram. Use consistent bins when comparing distributions and label the count or density axis. |
          #|      #|| How are two measurements related? | Scatter plot. Use one point per observation; correlation alone does not establish cause. |
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Label axes with units and give enough context to interpret the data, such as the
          #|      #|period and source. Use an available plotting tool such as Matplotlib for a static
          #|      #|plot, save it, and reference it as a file artifact.
          #|      #|If changing inputs or filters is part of the answer, use an interactive HTML
          #|      #|artifact instead. See `artifacts` for storage, references, and interactive answers.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/diago" => (
          #|      #|# Diago / D2: architecture and dependencies
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `diago` or `d2` fence; both use the same bundled Diago renderer. Diago
          #|      #|supports a subset of D2, so start with basic nodes, edges, and containers rather
          #|      #|than relying on syntax from a different D2 renderer.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- `id: Label` defines a node; `a -> b: Label` is a directed connection and
          #|      #|  `a -- b` is undirected. For imports, state that arrows point from importer to
          #|      #|  dependency. For data flow, label arrows with the operation or data they carry.
          #|      #|- `direction: right` lays out left to right; `down` lays out top to bottom.
          #|      #|- Braces hold attributes or child nodes. Use containers for real boundaries,
          #|      #|  such as an owning service, and dotted IDs to connect across them.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Architecture across a boundary
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```diago
          #|      #|direction: right
          #|      #|desktop: Desktop {
          #|      #|  panel: File panel
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|cloud: Cloud {
          #|      #|  api: Cloud API
          #|      #|  storage: File store {
          #|      #|    shape: cylinder
          #|      #|  }
          #|      #|  api -> storage: Read original bytes
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|desktop.panel -> cloud.api: Request download
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/katex" => (
          #|      #|# Drawing flowcharts with KaTeX block math
          #|      #|
          #|      #|KaTeX can draw a flowchart from ordinary boxes and arrows, with text, formulas,
          #|      #|or both as labels. The same technique works for a few connected components.
          #|      #|For specific diagram types and features beyond KaTeX, see the
          #|      #|`diagrams` guide.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|In SeekMoon, put `$$` on separate opening and closing lines, with no code fence
          #|      #|around the block.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## A short workflow
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Make a node with `\boxed{...}` and put prose inside `\text{...}`. Connect the
          #|      #|nodes with `\longrightarrow`, or use `\xrightarrow{...}` when an arrow needs a
          #|      #|label.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Submit request}} \longrightarrow
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Review request}} \longrightarrow
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Notify requester}}
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## A decision with formulas
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `\begin{array}{...}` to place nodes and arrows on a grid: `&` separates
          #|      #|columns and `\\` ends a row. Leave cells empty to line up the incoming arrow
          #|      #|with the decision. Label each outgoing arrow with its condition or outcome.
          #|      #|Here the two branches compute $y=|x|$.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|\begin{array}{ccccc}
          #|      #| & & \boxed{\text{Input }x} & & \\
          #|      #| & & \downarrow & & \\
          #|      #|\boxed{y=x} & \xleftarrow{\text{yes}} & \boxed{x\ge 0\,?} &
          #|      #|\xrightarrow{\text{no}} & \boxed{y=-x}
          #|      #|\end{array}
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Keep the grid narrow enough to read in the transcript. KaTeX does not route
          #|      #|edges or rearrange the chart as it grows, and does not run TikZ.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/mermaid" => (
          #|      #|# Mermaid: flows and states
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `mermaid` fence. Pick a `flowchart` when the reader needs to follow actions
          #|      #|and decisions. Pick `stateDiagram-v2` when the reader needs to understand which
          #|      #|states are possible and which events cause transitions. If the project already
          #|      #|uses Mermaid and the point is message order, use `sequenceDiagram` instead of
          #|      #|trying to express the order in a flowchart.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Workflow and branches
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `flowchart TD` for top-down or `flowchart LR` for left-to-right layout. Give
          #|      #|nodes short IDs and readable labels. Label the outgoing edges of decisions with
          #|      #|the condition or outcome.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mermaid
          #|      #|flowchart TD
          #|      #|  Request[Download requested] --> Cache{Cached bytes ready?}
          #|      #|  Cache -->|Yes| Save[Save file]
          #|      #|  Cache -->|No| Fetch[Fetch original bytes]
          #|      #|  Fetch --> Save
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## State transitions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Label arrows with the event that changes state, not the name of the new state.
          #|      #|Use `[*]` for the initial or terminal point.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mermaid
          #|      #|stateDiagram-v2
          #|      #|  [*] --> Pending
          #|      #|  Pending --> Downloading: start
          #|      #|  Downloading --> Pending: retry
          #|      #|  Downloading --> Complete: saved
          #|      #|  Complete --> [*]
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/plantuml" => (
          #|      #|# PlantUML: interactions and type relationships
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `plantuml` or `uml` fence, with `@startuml` and `@enduml` inside it.
          #|      #|SeekMoon renders a subset of PlantUML; don't assume every extension or layout
          #|      #|option in the standalone renderer is available.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Sequence diagram
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a sequence diagram when the order of calls or responses matters. Declare the
          #|      #|participants, list messages from top to bottom, and label replies that affect
          #|      #|what happens next.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```plantuml
          #|      #|@startuml
          #|      #|participant Desktop
          #|      #|participant Cloud
          #|      #|Desktop -> Cloud : Request download
          #|      #|Cloud --> Desktop : Original file bytes
          #|      #|@enduml
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Class diagram
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a class diagram when the distinction between an interface, an implementation,
          #|      #|and their relationships is the point. Include only members relevant to the
          #|      #|question; use Diago (see `diagrams/diago`) for module-level dependencies instead.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```plantuml
          #|      #|@startuml
          #|      #|interface FileStore {
          #|      #|  + read(id)
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|class DiskStore
          #|      #|FileStore <|.. DiskStore
          #|      #|@enduml
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "frontend-rabbita" => (
          #|      #|# Rabbita Frontend and Full-Stack Applications
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use Rabbita for MoonBit browser UIs unless the project or user selects another
          #|      #|framework. Start with the project's README, `moon.mod`, and entry packages.
          #|      #|Frontend API notes use Rabbita 0.16.3; CLI commands were checked with Warren
          #|      #|0.4.0. Inspect the project's installed interfaces with `moon ide doc "@rabbita"`
          #|      #|before adapting examples.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Start a project
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use [Warren] for scaffolding, browser preview, and release builds:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```sh
          #|      #|moon install moonbit-community/warren
          #|      #|warren new my-app
          #|      #|cd my-app
          #|      #|warren dev --browser-entry main
          #|      #|# Release output: dist/
          #|      #|warren build --browser-entry main
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Warren defaults to `cmd/browser`; the browser-only template above uses `main`.
          #|      #|Read the generated README and `warren <command> --help` for the installed version.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## State, views, and effects
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Build components as functions returning `Val[Html]`. Use
          #|      #|  `create_pure_state(initial, update=(model, msg) => new_model)` for pure state,
          #|      #|  or `create_state(initial, update=(model, msg, emit) => (new_model, cmd))`
          #|      #|  for effects. Both return `(Val[Model], Emit[Msg])` and require `Model : Eq`.
          #|      #|  Keep state immutable; use derived values instead of duplicating state. See
          #|      #|  the [introduction] and [API signatures].
          #|      #|- Render with `model.view(...)` and `moonbit-community/rabbita/html` helpers.
          #|      #|  Bind messages through handlers such as `on_click=emit(Clicked)`;
          #|      #|  `emit(msg)` produces a `Cmd`. Return commands from updates or attach them to
          #|      #|  handlers: constructing and discarding a command does not execute it.
          #|      #|  Keep rendering free of side effects. See [commands].
          #|      #|- Mount a client-rendered app with `@rabbita.new(app).mount("app")`; the host
          #|      #|  HTML must contain an element with that id. Prefer library APIs over raw JS
          #|      #|  FFI, and keep callbacks, commands, and requests out of the model, following
          #|      #|  the [upstream guidance].
          #|      #|- Use `moonbit-community/rabbita/http` for requests. Complete a request with
          #|      #|  `expect_text`, `expect_json`, or `expect_empty`, and return its command from
          #|      #|  the update. Callbacks should send result messages; updates handle loading,
          #|      #|  success, and failure. Use `with_json` for JSON request bodies. See [HTTP].
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Full-stack applications
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Start from [fullstack-moonbit], a Rabbita + Moonback Todo app with SSR and
          #|      #|hydration. Preserve its pinned dependencies and read its README first:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```sh
          #|      #|git clone https://github.com/moonbit-community/fullstack-moonbit.git my-app
          #|      #|cd my-app
          #|      #|warren dev
          #|      #|# Build browser assets and a native server into dist/
          #|      #|warren build --server-target native
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Follow its package boundaries: `cmd/browser` targets `js`; `cmd/server` and
          #|      #|`shared/api` target `native+wasm`; `shared/components` supports all three.
          #|      #|The server passes the shared root component and API routes to `rabbita/server`;
          #|      #|the browser calls `@rabbita.new(@components.app).hydrate()` for that component.
          #|      #|Use this paired setup for SSR (experimental), rather than replacing hydration
          #|      #|with a fresh DOM mount. Put shared data types and validation in packages both
          #|      #|sides can import; validate again on the server, as shown in the bundled
          #|      #|tutorial at `<bundled-resources>/doc/moonbit/tutorial/fullstack-one-project.md`.
          #|      #|Keep browser-only and server-only IO out of shared UI/domain packages.
          #|      #|For native desktop hosting, also read the `desktop-proton` guide.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon check --target js`, check the backend's target when present, and test
          #|      #|changed state/validation logic. Build with the selected Warren entries, then
          #|      #|check rendering, interactions, and request success/failure in a browser.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|[Warren]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/warren/README.md
          #|      #|[introduction]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/doc/001_intro/readme.mbt.md
          #|      #|[API signatures]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/rabbita/pkg.generated.mbti
          #|      #|[commands]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/doc/004_using_command/readme.mbt.md
          #|      #|[HTTP]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/doc/005_http/readme.mbt.md
          #|      #|[upstream guidance]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/rabbita/blob/d31819b4b59653550af5fcd3c1bc5c424caae64a/skills/rabbita.md
          #|      #|[fullstack-moonbit]: https://github.com/moonbit-community/fullstack-moonbit/tree/ad1759d6209af97523679ac58b25b407847de1e6
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "git-shipping" => (
          #|      #|# Git authorship and shipping
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Attribute Git work you author:
          #|      #|  - End every commit message you write with
          #|      #|    `Co-Authored-By: SeekMoon <seekmoon@moonbitlang.com>` as its own final
          #|      #|    paragraph. Pass the trailer as a separate `-m` argument or preserve the
          #|      #|    blank line when writing the message from a file.
          #|      #|  - End every pull request description you write with this final line:
          #|      #|    `Generated with SeekMoon`
          #|      #|  - Add each marker only once, and never add it to work someone else wrote.
          #|      #|- Attach every pull request opened in this conversation — by you, a script,
          #|      #|  or a workflow you ran — once, when the `seekmoon` MCP server is available
          #|      #|  (`@mcp.search(server="seekmoon")` says whether it is):
          #|      #|  `@mcp.call("seekmoon", "artifacts_attach", { "type": "pull_request", "url": pr_url })`.
          #|      #|  When the server is unavailable, skip this step.
          #|      #|- Read a failing CI check by its run id: `gh pr checks <n>` prints each
          #|      #|  check with its run URL — take the id from the failing one (or run
          #|      #|  `git rev-parse HEAD` first, then `gh run list --commit <sha>
          #|      #|  --status failure --limit 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId'`), then
          #|      #|  `gh run view <run-id> --log-failed`. Never pick "the latest run on the
          #|      #|  branch": a PR can trigger several workflows, and without an id the
          #|      #|  command opens an interactive picker nothing can answer. A red check you
          #|      #|  cannot explain is a finding to report, not a detail to omit.
          #|      #|- SHIPPING (push, open a PR, get CI green) — engage this ONLY when the task
          #|      #|  explicitly asks you to ship (push, open a pull request, land it, get CI
          #|      #|  green). Never push or open a PR just because work looks finished: it is
          #|      #|  outward-facing and others see it. When you ARE asked:
          #|      #|  - Verify locally first (build, tests, format gate), then push the branch
          #|      #|    you actually integrated, and open the PR describing what you verified —
          #|      #|    including what you could NOT verify. Never force-push a branch you do
          #|      #|    not own, and never merge unless asked to.
          #|      #|  - Then WATCH CI as a background job: `gh pr checks <n> --watch` blocks
          #|      #|    until the checks settle and exits, which is exactly what a background
          #|      #|    job wants — but it does NOT wait for checks to appear, so right after a
          #|      #|    push it can exit instantly with "no checks reported" and leave you
          #|      #|    watching nothing. Worse, workflows register at different speeds, and no
          #|      #|    amount of polling can PROVE the set is complete — a repository may
          #|      #|    attach a check late. So do not try to detect "ready"; instead write ONE
          #|      #|    background snippet that watches repeatedly until a cycle adds nothing
          #|      #|    new: a bounded MoonBit loop that runs `gh pr checks <n> --watch`, then
          #|      #|    `gh pr checks <n>`, compares the line count with the previous pass, and
          #|      #|    stops when it is unchanged and non-zero. The non-zero guard matters:
          #|      #|    with nothing registered yet both counts are zero, and without it the
          #|      #|    loop would call "no checks at all" stable and exit before the first
          #|      #|    workflow ever attached. Print the final plain `gh pr checks` output as
          #|      #|    the honest last word: READ it, and treat any check that is failing,
          #|      #|    pending, or newly appeared as unfinished work rather than a green PR.
          #|      #|    While the monitor runs, keep working or call `job_wait` with its job ID
          #|      #|    if you have no other work. The completion notice resumes that wait; an
          #|      #|    already-finished turn is not restarted. Read the result with `job_output`.
          #|      #|    Never call a PR done while a check is pending or unreported.
          #|      #|  - Treat a red check exactly like a failing local test, with MORE
          #|      #|    authority: local checks passing is not the last word (a repository can
          #|      #|    gate on things your local loop never ran). Read the failing run's log by
          #|      #|    its run id as described above, fix the cause on the branch, push again,
          #|      #|    and re-watch. Repeat until green.
          #|      #|  - Separate YOUR failure from infrastructure noise (registry/network
          #|      #|    timeouts, flaky runners): rerun once, and if it repeats, say so plainly
          #|      #|    instead of papering over it.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "markdown-replies" => (
          #|      #|SeekMoon Desktop renders math in Markdown replies with KaTeX. Use `$...$` for inline math and `$$...$$` for display math. Do not use `\(...\)` or `\[...\]` as math delimiters in Markdown.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "mcp" => (
          #|      #|# MCP services
          #|      #|
          #|      #|MCP servers can connect external services: chat, issue trackers, calendars,
          #|      #|documents, and more. Their tools are not in your function list, and
          #|      #|`@openseek_tools.call` does not reach them; a script uses the SDK's `mcp`
          #|      #|package. When a task may involve such a service, or before concluding that
          #|      #|something cannot be done, search the catalog and print the page:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/openseek_tools@0.4.2/mcp" @mcp,
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  // No arguments list every tool; `query` ranks by keywords; `server` lists
          #|      #|  // one server's tools after its instructions, or says why it is unavailable.
          #|      #|  let page = @mcp.search(query="list open issues")
          #|      #|  println(page.text)
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|After the search script returns, read the tool descriptions and schemas,
          #|      #|select a tool for the task, and construct its arguments in the next script.
          #|      #|Call it with `@mcp.call(server, tool, arguments)`. Check `is_error` first,
          #|      #|then match `data : Json?` against the discovered output schema and print only
          #|      #|the fields or summary needed. For example, if discovery showed a `tracker`
          #|      #|server with `list_issues`, a `repo` argument, and an `issues` result array:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/openseek_tools@0.4.2/mcp" @mcp,
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  let result = @mcp.call("tracker", "list_issues", { "repo": "alpha" })
          #|      #|  if result.is_error { fail(result.content) }
          #|      #|  guard result.data is Some({ "issues": Array(issues), .. }) else {
          #|      #|    fail("Expected structured issues. Tool text: \{result.content}")
          #|      #|  }
          #|      #|  println("Issues on this page: \{issues.length()}")
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`data` comes from the server's `structuredContent`; `content` renders its
          #|      #|content blocks as text. A tool can return either or both. `data` is absent
          #|      #|when the server supplies no structured result or it exceeds the reply budget.
          #|      #|Use `content` for text results. For a read that needs structured data, narrow
          #|      #|an oversized query or reduce its page size. Images are saved as files listed
          #|      #|in `images`; pass a path to `@openseek_tools.call("read_image", { "path": path })`
          #|      #|to view one. To apply a known operation across catalog entries, such as the
          #|      #|same check on every server, iterate over `page.tools` in one script. Each
          #|      #|record has `server`, `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, and `output_schema`.
          #|      #|A page holds a limited number of tools; its `next_offset` continues the listing.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|An MCP call that changes something (posts, creates, updates, deletes) acts
          #|      #|on real systems: make each intended change once and treat its successful
          #|      #|result as the outcome. Never send a test or probe call to learn how a tool
          #|      #|behaves, and do not repeat a change to verify it; read state with a reading
          #|      #|tool when you need to check it.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "moonbit-development" => (
          #|      #|# MoonBit development
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The following workflow applies when the task is MoonBit code.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Compiler-Driven Work
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Refactor and fix by compiler guidance, not by text. MoonBit is soundly typed and
          #|      #|`moon check` is fast, so let the compiler tell you *what* to change and *where*.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Loop: `moon check` (use `--output-json` or `--diagnostic-limit <N>` to group repeats, sync mode) →
          #|      #|fix the reported `path:line`s with `edit`/`multi_edit` →
          #|      #|re-check until clean.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|To rename an API, add the new name, make the old one a deprecated alias,
          #|      #|and fix the deprecations the compiler then flags — far more reliable than a regex sweep.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon check` through `mbtx` (for example, `filename="@builtin/check.mbtx"`) as the primary fast feedback loop;
          #|      #|add `--diagnostic-limit 5` for focused diagnostics. It skips code
          #|      #|generation, so it is way faster than `moon build` or `moon test`. Use
          #|      #|`moon build` or `moon test` only when you need artifacts or test results.
          #|      #|After `edit` or `write` changes `moon.mod`, `moon.pkg`, `moon.work`, `.mbt`,
          #|      #|or `.mbt.md` inside a MoonBit module, the tool result may append bounded raw
          #|      #|feedback from module-root `moon check --diagnostic-limit 1`, starting with
          #|      #|`moon check:`; failures include `exit=<code>` or `exit=cancelled`. Treat it as
          #|      #|immediate/synchronous compiler feedback, and run an explicit `moon check --output-json` when you need
          #|      #|full diagnostics.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Common `moon` subcommands
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- `moon check`: type-check for compiler feedback; supports
          #|      #|  `--target` and `--diagnostic-limit <N>`. A successful check also updates
          #|      #|  `.mbti` files for checked packages whose preferred backend is covered.
          #|      #|- `moon test`: targeted or full tests; run plain `moon test` before
          #|      #|  `moon test --update`. Example: `moon test parser --filter "Parser::*"
          #|      #|  --diagnostic-limit 5`. Filters support glob syntax. This is THE way to
          #|      #|  exercise local package code: write a black-box `_test.mbt` test and run it
          #|      #|  with `--filter` — never probe local packages through `moon run -e`.
          #|      #|- `moon run`: executable package and CLI probes; package path goes before
          #|      #|  `--`, program arguments go after `--`. Example:
          #|      #|  `moon run --target native cmd/tomljson -- input.toml` (a file the `write` tool
          #|      #|  put in the workspace).
          #|      #|- `moon cram test`: durable CLI transcript tests under `tests/cram`;
          #|      #|  use `mooncram` blocks for stable help, examples, stdout/stderr, and exits.
          #|      #|  Example: `moon cram test tests/cram`.
          #|      #|- `moon fmt`: format MoonBit sources before finishing. Example:
          #|      #|  `moon fmt --check parser`.
          #|      #|- `moon build`: check build artifacts or backend-specific builds. Example:
          #|      #|  `moon build --target native cmd/tool --diagnostic-limit 5`.
          #|      #|- `moon doc` and `moon explain`: documentation and diagnostic help.
          #|      #|- `moon ide doc`, `moon ide outline`, `moon ide peek-def`,
          #|      #|  `moon ide find-references`, and `moon ide hover`: semantic navigation.
          #|      #|  Verified examples: `moon ide doc "@json.parse"`,
          #|      #|  `moon ide outline parser`, `moon ide peek-def parse --loc
          #|      #|  src/parser.mbt:42:9`, `moon ide find-references parse --loc
          #|      #|  src/parser.mbt:42:9`, and `moon ide hover parse --loc src/parser.mbt:42:9`.
          #|      #|- `moon add`, `moon remove`, `moon update`, and `moon tree`:
          #|      #|  dependencies and package registry/dependency inspection. Examples:
          #|      #|  `moon add moonbitlang/x`, `moon remove moonbitlang/x`,
          #|      #|  `moon update`, `moon tree`.
          #|      #|- `moon search`: search the package registry for modules by keyword when
          #|      #|  the exact module name is not known yet; then add the chosen module with
          #|      #|  `moon add`. Examples: `moon search base64`,
          #|      #|  `moon search json --limit 5 --json` (`--json` prints one result set as
          #|      #|  JSON for parsing).
          #|      #|- `moon clean`: clear `_build` when stale build output is suspected.
          #|      #|  Example: `moon clean`.
          #|      #|- `moon bench`: run benchmarks, e.g. `moon bench lib/parser`.
          #|      #|- `moon coverage analyze`: inspect test coverage when coverage matters.
          #|      #|  Example: `moon coverage analyze --package user/project/parser`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Ast based search with `moongrep`
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```text
          #|      #|moonx moonbit-community/moongrep scan lib/parser --pattern 'match $(v:exp) { Some($(x:id)) => $(a:exp); None => $(b:exp) }' --json
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The positional argument is the scan root (a directory or one `.mbt` file;
          #|      #|the default `.` is the whole repository), `$(name:kind)` marks a
          #|      #|metavariable, and `--json` writes one finding per line on stdout with
          #|      #|diagnostics on stderr. Pass each argument as its own element of
          #|      #|`@shell.Cmd("moonx", [...])` — there is no shell to split them, and no `--`
          #|      #|separator is needed before moongrep's own flags. The pattern language, output
          #|      #|fields, scan-root exclusions, and exit codes are in
          #|      #|the `moongrep` guide; read it before going beyond this
          #|      #|example rather than guessing the flags.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## MoonBit Project Setup
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Current MoonBit modules use `moon.mod`.
          #|      #|- Create `moon.mod` before running `moon check`; otherwise `moon` may walk up to
          #|      #|  an unrelated parent module.
          #|      #|- `moon.mod` is the module manifest: keep module `name`, `version`,
          #|      #|  module-level dependency versions, `warnings`, and module options there.
          #|      #|- Packages are directories with `moon.pkg`.
          #|      #|- `moon.pkg` is the package manifest: keep package imports, import aliases,
          #|      #|  test/wbtest imports, `supported_targets`, and package declarations there.
          #|      #|  Do not put package imports or aliases in `moon.mod`; do not put module
          #|      #|  dependency versions in `moon.pkg`.
          #|      #|- `_test.mbt` files are black-box tests. Use `_wbtest.mbt` only when tests must
          #|      #|  inspect private helpers.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Async IO is part of the standard library. Import the packages you use, such
          #|      #|as `moonbitlang/core/async/fs`, in `moon.pkg`. They need no registry dependency.
          #|      #|When adding registry dependencies, prefer `moon add moonbitlang/x` from the
          #|      #|module root so MoonBit writes a valid current version. Do not guess dependency
          #|      #|versions by hand.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|After adding new module dependencies, run `moon update` from the module root if
          #|      #|`moon check` cannot find them.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## API Lookup And Probes
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Use `moon ide doc` before guessing unfamiliar APIs. Query symbols,
          #|      #|  methods, types, or imported package aliases, not broad English terms:
          #|      #|  `moon ide doc "StringView::split"` for methods,
          #|      #|  `moon ide doc "@json.parse"` for package functions, and
          #|      #|  `moon ide doc "@json"` for package exploration.
          #|      #|- `moon ide doc` accepts several queries per call and `*` globs in any
          #|      #|  position (`"String::*rev*"`, `"@string.*parse*"`, `"*parse*"`). When
          #|      #|  unsure of a name, batch candidates with a bare glob in one call —
          #|      #|  `moon ide doc "parse_float" "*parse*" "@string"` — misses report
          #|      #|  `No results found` inline while the others return. Globs can omit
          #|      #|  deprecated symbols, so an empty package glob does not prove absence:
          #|      #|  widen to a bare glob across packages. On a miss, never retry
          #|      #|  near-identical spellings or compile-probe blindly: re-query once with a
          #|      #|  bare glob or list the package and read the real names. Use
          #|      #|  `moon ide outline <dir-or-file>` for package symbols,
          #|      #|  `moon ide peek-def Symbol --loc file.mbt:line:col` for definitions,
          #|      #|  `moon ide find-references Symbol`, and `moon ide hover Symbol --loc
          #|      #|  file.mbt:line:col` for types.
          #|      #|- Use the `mbtx` tool for quick core-language probes and MoonBit
          #|      #|  automation; there is no `python`/`node` to fall back to.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Validation Before Finish
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Before finishing code work:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|1. Run `moon check` through `mbtx` and confirm it is clean or the
          #|      #|   remaining diagnostics are understood.
          #|      #|2. Run targeted `moon test`.
          #|      #|3. Run `moon fmt` when formatting may have changed.
          #|      #|4. Run task-specific acceptance probes with `moon run`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use exact `moon` subcommands for final validation: `moon check` for fast
          #|      #|type-checking, `moon test` for tests, `moon run` for CLI probes, `moon cram
          #|      #|test tests/cram` for durable CLI transcript fixtures, `moon fmt` for formatting,
          #|      #|and `moon build` for build artifacts.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For CLI work, run probes that cover:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- file arguments;
          #|      #|- stdin mode;
          #|      #|- invalid input and exit/error behavior;
          #|      #|- stdout shape for successful output.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When CLI behavior should become a lasting fixture, add `tests/cram/*.md`
          #|      #|coverage with `mooncram` blocks and run `moon cram test tests/cram`. Keep
          #|      #|live or networked CLI tests opt-in, for example under `tests/live`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Report the commands actually run and any remaining caveats.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "moongrep" => (
          #|      #|# Structural search with `moongrep`
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`moonx` runs `moongrep`, a structural (AST-based) search and lint tool for
          #|      #|MoonBit. Use it when a text grep cannot express the shape you need ("all
          #|      #|`inspect` calls whose `content` is a literal", "every `match` over
          #|      #|`Some`/`None`", "every call of one function nested inside another"). No `--`
          #|      #|separator is needed: the arguments after the coordinate are moongrep's own.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The system prompt carries the simplest shape — one `match` over `Some`/`None`.
          #|      #|This one covers more of the language in a single call: a nested application,
          #|      #|two `id` captures, `$_` for the arguments nobody cares about, `--guard` to
          #|      #|keep only the inner callees whose name matches a regex, and `--exclude` for
          #|      #|the one directory the defaults miss here.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```text
          #|      #|moonx moonbit-community/moongrep scan . --exclude editor/codemirror/demo --pattern '$(outer:id)($_, $(inner:id)($_))' --guard '{$inner: "^error_result$"}' --json
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The same call from a snippet — each argument is its own array element, because
          #|      #|`@shell.Cmd` starts the program directly and no shell is there to split a
          #|      #|string:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/core/async/shell",
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  // A whole-repo scan can match more than `.output()` is able to capture, so
          #|      #|  // stream the records instead. `each_line` receives stdout only: the child's
          #|      #|  // stderr, where the skip warnings go, passes through to this program's own
          #|      #|  // stderr.
          #|      #|  let mut findings = 0
          #|      #|  let code = @shell.Cmd("moonx", [
          #|      #|    "moonbit-community/moongrep", "scan", ".",
          #|      #|    "--exclude", "editor/codemirror/demo",
          #|      #|    "--pattern", "$(outer:id)($_, $(inner:id)($_))",
          #|      #|    "--guard", "{$inner: \"^error_result$\"}",
          #|      #|    "--json",
          #|      #|  ]).each_line(line => {
          #|      #|    findings += 1
          #|      #|    // Keep the printed excerpt bounded; count the rest.
          #|      #|    if findings <= 20 {
          #|      #|      println(line)
          #|      #|    }
          #|      #|  })
          #|      #|  println("exit=\{code} findings=\{findings}")
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Scan roots and exclusions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`scan` (and `lint`, which prepends the embedded builtin rules) scans
          #|      #|recursively from the scan root — a directory or a single `.mbt` file; the
          #|      #|default root `.` means the whole repository, which is the typical use.
          #|      #|Hidden entries — any child name beginning with `.` (case-independent) —
          #|      #|plus `_build`, `node_modules`, and `target` are skipped by default;
          #|      #|`--exclude <name-or-path>` skips more entries. These exclusions apply to
          #|      #|child entries during traversal: an explicitly selected scan root is still
          #|      #|inspected even when its final path component matches one of the rules.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Non-hidden tool/eval/worktree directories are NOT in the default exclusions:
          #|      #|a whole-repo scan descends into them and duplicates findings across nested
          #|      #|checkouts, or aborts on a dangling symlink inside one. Hidden ones
          #|      #|(`.claude`, `.worktrees`, `.moonagent`, `.repos`) are already covered by the
          #|      #|leading-dot rule; exclude the rest up front — in this checkout:
          #|      #|`--exclude editor/codemirror/demo`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Output
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `--json` (not `--output-json`, which is `moon check`'s spelling) for
          #|      #|agent-friendly output: one JSON object per line on stdout,
          #|      #|`{"type": "finding", ...}`, carrying `file` (relative to the scan root, often
          #|      #|`./`-prefixed), `rule_id`, `description`, `range` (1-based `line`/`column`),
          #|      #|`matched_source`, and `source_context`. Diagnostics such as skipped blocks go
          #|      #|to stderr as `{"type": "warning", ...}` lines. A scan with zero findings
          #|      #|writes nothing and still exits 0.
          #|      #|When parsing records with `@json.parse`, integer fields such as
          #|      #|`range.start.line` pattern-match as `Number(value)` (a `Double`; call
          #|      #|`.to_int()` on it), not `Int`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|A whole-repo scan can match many nodes — `@shell.Cmd(...).output()` cannot
          #|      #|capture unbounded output, so a large scan hits the shell's output-limit error
          #|      #|— stream with `.each_line()` (one record per callback line) to count or
          #|      #|aggregate findings. The two streams stay separate either way: `.output()`
          #|      #|keeps them apart as `out.stdout()` and `out.stderr()` (sharing one capture
          #|      #|budget), and `.each_line()` streams stdout alone, leaving the warnings to the
          #|      #|snippet's own stderr. To read the warnings back inside the snippet, use
          #|      #|`.output()` and inspect `out.stderr()`, or redirect them with
          #|      #|`stderr=ToFile(...)`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`--rules <dir>` and `--rule <file>` load YAML rule files, `--disable
          #|      #|<rule-id>` drops a loaded rule, and `--verbose` traces traversal on stderr.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Patterns
          #|      #|
          #|      #|A `--pattern` is a MoonBit *expression* containing `$(name:kind)`
          #|      #|metavariables — `exp`, `id`, `const`, `arg`, `pat`, `type` — plus `$_` to
          #|      #|match anything without capturing. Matching is on the untyped CST: whitespace
          #|      #|and comments do not matter, but syntax shape does (`Some(1)` does not match
          #|      #|`Some($(some:id))`; `Ok`/`Err` branches do not match a `Some`/`None`
          #|      #|pattern). A metavariable used twice must capture equal structure. `--guard
          #|      #|'{$name: "regex"}'` filters `id` and `const` captures (substring match unless
          #|      #|anchored with `^...$`).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Labeled arguments match by label — `inspect($_, content=$(c:const))` finds
          #|      #|the `inspect` calls whose expectation is a literal. `const` covers ordinary
          #|      #|literals only: a multi-line `#|` string is not one, so the snapshot tests
          #|      #|written that way need `content=$_`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When a pattern surprises you,
          #|      #|`moonx moonbit-community/moongrep dump --expr '...'` or
          #|      #|`moonx moonbit-community/moongrep dump --impl 'fn f { ... }'` prints the CST
          #|      #|of a snippet. `moonx moonbit-community/moongrep docs RuleSpec` and
          #|      #|`moonx moonbit-community/moongrep docs CLISpec` print the full specs.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Lint rules
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The builtin `lint` rules are advisory style checks — `catch_all`, for
          #|      #|instance, flags deliberate `catch { _ => () }` swallows. Treat rule counts as
          #|      #|signal, not a bug list, and read `matched_source` before acting on a finding.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Exit codes
          #|      #|
          #|      #|0 for help, dump, findings, no-findings, and parse warnings (no-match is NOT
          #|      #|grep's 1); 2 usage; 3 invalid dump input; 4 rule-source failure; 5 invalid
          #|      #|rule content (including a `--pattern` that is not a valid MoonBit
          #|      #|expression); 6 missing/unreadable scan input; 7 output failure.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The scanner follows symlinks, so a broken symlink anywhere under the scan
          #|      #|root aborts the whole scan with 6. Source blocks whose syntax the bundled
          #|      #|parser does not know yet (e.g. nested record-spread `is` patterns) are
          #|      #|skipped with a stderr warning while exit stays 0 — treat skipped blocks as a
          #|      #|blind spot.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "presentation" => (
          #|      #|# Presenting files, interactive answers, and sources
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- When an answer cites a real local file, link it as
          #|      #|  [main.mbt](absolute-path): plain label, the target is the file's absolute
          #|      #|  path, optional line number inside the target, angle brackets around a
          #|      #|  target with spaces (`[My Report.md](<absolute path with spaces>:3>)`), no
          #|      #|  backticks in or around the link, no `file://` or other URI schemes, no line
          #|      #|  ranges.
          #|      #|- Use the workspace-file fence only when a file already exists in the active
          #|      #|  workspace and the user should open it as a separate deliverable, such as a
          #|      #|  generated report, spreadsheet, or image. It renders as a file card in the
          #|      #|  desktop transcript and exported viewer:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  ```openseek-file
          #|      #|  reports/prompt_leak_red_team_matrix.xlsx
          #|      #|  ```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  Put exactly one path relative to the active workspace root on the line
          #|      #|  inside the fence. Do not use an absolute path, `file://`, `~`, or a path
          #|      #|  containing `..`; do not put file contents or multiple paths in this fence.
          #|      #|  Do not use this syntax for a file that has not been created or whose
          #|      #|  workspace location is unknown. When the user needs the contents in the
          #|      #|  answer, provide them directly; when citing a particular source file or line,
          #|      #|  use the ordinary Markdown link form above.
          #|      #|  Session-generated files use paths such as `scratch/artifact/answer.html` in
          #|      #|  this same fence. Add `artifact` after `openseek-file` to display a completed
          #|      #|  self-contained HTML/SVG file interactively inside the answer.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Workspace File Previews in SeekMoon
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When the user asks to view or preview a workspace file, or you finish a
          #|      #|standalone visual deliverable such as an HTML page, PDF, image, or Office
          #|      #|document, discover `preview_open` on the `seekmoon` MCP server and call it
          #|      #|before your final response, unless the user requests otherwise. Save generated
          #|      #|files first and pass the path relative to the workspace root. Keep a file
          #|      #|reference in your response.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Routine source-code edits do not require automatic preview. Inline interactive
          #|      #|artifacts retain their existing inline rendering. If the tool is unavailable or
          #|      #|fails, provide the file reference and briefly report the limitation. The result
          #|      #|`opening` acknowledges acceptance, not successful rendering.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Inline Diagrams in SeekMoon
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When a diagram would make a workflow or relationship easier to understand,
          #|      #|read the `diagrams` guide to choose a format.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Generated File Artifacts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For conversation artifacts, write completed files under `<session scratch>/artifact/`.
          #|      #|Use the actual scratch path shown in Environment and pass output paths explicitly
          #|      #|to scripts. Use new filenames for revisions.
          #|      #|Reference the file with an `openseek-file` fence containing
          #|      #|`scratch/artifact/FILENAME`. Use `openseek-file artifact` when the HTML/SVG should
          #|      #|serve as an interactive part of the answer. Ordinary references display file cards;
          #|      #|read the `artifacts` guide for storage, references and packaging.
          #|      #|Save user-requested project files at the requested workspace paths.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Interactive Answers
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Interaction can itself answer a question. In chats with interactive previews,
          #|      #|proactively offer a small component when seeing, exploring, comparing or
          #|      #|providing information interactively would help the user. Choose controls that
          #|      #|fit the task, including a short form when it makes related details easier to
          #|      #|provide. Reuse what the user has provided, label fields and mark optional ones.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Make the component useful in its initial state, with a meaningful starting
          #|      #|result for explorations.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Read the `artifacts` guide and the starter sources before
          #|      #|authoring. Write ordinary MoonBit/Rabbita source with local state and events.
          #|      #|The same starter illustrates interactive charts with mathplotlib when useful.
          #|      #|The guide covers `init.mbtx`, batch edits with `write.mbtx`, explicit scratch
          #|      #|paths and packaging with `build.mbtx`. Check the application, run relevant model
          #|      #|tests and reference the completed page with the fence returned by the packager.
          #|      #|Keep numerical examples consistent with the tested model and give their inputs.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Components may offer JSON state through the preview bridge. The user attaches
          #|      #|it to the composer and sends a follow-up. `<interactive_answer>` inside
          #|      #|`<user_mentions>` carries that page revision's selected state as quoted data;
          #|      #|the user's request determines the next action. When a selection suggests a
          #|      #|specific question, offer a clearly labelled button that requests a follow-up
          #|      #|through the bridge for the user to confirm.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Numbered Source Citations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- When an answer relies on inspected documents, workspace files, or web
          #|      #|  sources, cite the supporting source immediately after the claim with a
          #|      #|  standard Markdown footnote: `Revenue increased by 12%.[^1]`. Agent-authored
          #|      #|  citations do not require the user to select document text first. Do not
          #|      #|  force citations into conversational replies or claims that need no source.
          #|      #|- Start positive integer labels at `1` in each answer, ordered by first use.
          #|      #|  Reuse the same number for the same source and location. Put one definition
          #|      #|  for each cited number at the end of that answer, after a blank line:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  ```markdown
          #|      #|  Revenue increased by 12%.[^1] The calculation follows the documented rule.[^2]
          #|      #|  The report also explains the assumptions.[^1]
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  [^1]: [Annual report](/workspace/reports/annual.pdf#page=12) — p. 12, Revenue.
          #|      #|  [^2]: [Calculation guide](https://example.com/calculation) — Growth rate.
          #|      #|  ```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Each definition starts with a descriptive Markdown link to the actual
          #|      #|  source. Follow it with a short location or context when useful: PDF page,
          #|      #|  Word heading, Excel sheet and cell range, or web section. Local file link
          #|      #|  destinations follow the file-link rules above; code may include a line.
          #|      #|  For verified Office locations, use these app-supported fragments so clicking
          #|      #|  the source navigates and highlights it: PDF `#page=12` (one-based physical
          #|      #|  page), Word `#heading=Revenue` (exact paragraph text, unique in the preview)
          #|      #|  or `#paragraph=%2Fdocx%2Fbody%2Fp%5B3%5D` (an inspected office selector), and
          #|      #|  Excel `#sheet=Revenue&range=B2:D8`. Percent-encode each heading, selector, or
          #|      #|  sheet value separately, including spaces, non-ASCII text, `&`, `#`, and `%`.
          #|      #|  Keep the readable location after the link. Do not invent a fragment, use
          #|      #|  code-line suffixes for Office, or infer a source selector from a visual
          #|      #|  paragraph index. Omit the fragment if the exact location is unknown.
          #|      #|  Different source locations may have separate numbers.
          #|      #|- Cite only sources actually inspected in the current task, using their real
          #|      #|  paths/URLs and verified locations. A citation must support its associated
          #|      #|  claim. Distinguish source statements from your own inference. Do not invent
          #|      #|  a source, page, cell range, or quotation; omit unknown location metadata.
          #|      #|- Include only definitions referenced in the answer. Do not wrap citations in
          #|      #|  code, use raw HTML, or add a second manually numbered bibliography. The
          #|      #|  desktop renders the inline numbers and a consolidated Sources section from
          #|      #|  these definitions. Ordinary links remain appropriate for navigation and
          #|      #|  deliverables that are not evidence citations.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/design" => (
          #|      #|# Presentation Design Guidelines
          #|      #|
          #|      #|**General principle**: Skip any step below if the model does not support it. Any specification from the user—template, brand guidelines, color scheme, fonts, reference styles, attachments, and so on—takes precedence over this guide and any design system document.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 1. Understanding the Requirements
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Clarify the requirements**: Determine the purpose, audience, page count, and style direction, as well as the input type (topic only / full document / page-by-page outline and script).
          #|      #|- **Determine the page count**: Follow the user's specification first; otherwise align with the page-by-page outline or script; if neither exists, infer it from the topic and the amount of content, and explain your reasoning.
          #|      #|- **Do not interrupt by default**: When requirements are unclear, proceed with reasonable defaults; ask questions only for hard blockers such as an attachment that cannot be opened or contradictory intent.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 2. Design System and Color Scheme
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Choose a scenario**: Select the primary scenario based on the audience and the reader's task: analytical decision-making / business proposal / management report / academic research / education and training / technical engineering / brand creativity. Add a secondary scenario when necessary; if nothing matches, use a general-purpose approach rather than forcing a fit. For tasks that reproduce an image/PDF in Typst, analyze the original document directly without choosing a scenario.
          #|      #|- **Unified design system**: Use a single set of color, font, whitespace, and layout rules throughout the presentation; do not introduce conflicting constraints midway.
          #|      #|- **Color scheme priority**: User specification > the organization's primary color > the design system's recommendation. When the user states their organization, search for its logo first and extract the primary color.
          #|      #|- **Color constraints**: Avoid stacking red, purple, yellow, and green at the same time; unless specified by the user or a skill, use no more than three colors throughout. Text and background must have clear contrast.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 3. Fonts and Symbols
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Language and punctuation**: Default to Simplified Chinese unless the user requests Traditional; use full-width punctuation for Chinese, and half-width for English words, numbers, code, URLs, paths, technical identifiers, and mathematical content.
          #|      #|- **Font choice**: Choose fonts by language and tone. Use sans-serif for general reports; serif for academic, cultural, and formal presentations; rounded fonts for children's and educational scenarios; and monospaced fonts for code, number alignment, and metrics.
          #|      #|- **Font hierarchy**: Clearly define the division of labor between display and body fonts, unify the size baselines for headings, body text, captions, and sources, and establish hierarchy through size and weight rather than color.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 4. Content Organization and Page Layout
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Page task and title**: Before designing, clarify what each page should make the reader understand, believe, decide, or do; make titles state a judgment and avoid empty labels such as "Work Report", "Overview", or "Background".
          #|      #|- **Pacing**: Decide based on the content whether to include a table of contents page and section pages, balancing quick scanning with close reading of key points.
          #|      #|- **Table of contents layout**: Avoid stacking small text or leaving large blank areas; spread section blocks evenly across the page; if a single column does not fit, switch to two columns, arranging numbers and names separately.
          #|      #|- **Layout and alignment**: Choose the layout based on the content; do not default to thirds, quarters, or a 2×2 matrix. When using a horizontally parallel layout at the same level, default to equal widths with uniform alignment; use different widths only when you need to express a primary/secondary relationship.
          #|      #|- **Expressing hierarchy**: Unless the user requests otherwise, do not use rectangles or rounded cards to build hierarchy or achieve alignment; use line segments, whitespace, and contrast in font size and weight instead.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 5. Data, Charts, and Sources
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Do not fabricate**: Do not fabricate data, citations, customer cases, experimental results, or sources; mark missing materials as placeholders, assumptions, or to be supplemented.
          #|      #|- **Source attribution**: Pages involving external facts and data must state the source, date or time range, and methodology.
          #|      #|- **Structured presentation**: Use native tables or chart components for data comparisons, metric lists, parameter descriptions, and dimension breakdowns; do not use Markdown tables.
          #|      #|- **Legend legibility**: The colors of each legend in pie and donut charts must be easy to distinguish; you may use the primary color or a gradient of opacity.
          #|      #|- **Chart styling**: Check whether the default style is clear and consistent with the design system; adjust inappropriate axes, grid lines, legend boxes, and borders, and remove rainbow color schemes, 3D, gradients, and shadow effects that interfere with reading.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 6. Images and Assets
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Image usage**: Place images as the content requires and avoid overuse; cover images must be high quality. Do not use abstract shapes as decoration on the cover, closing, or section pages; when visual assets are needed, prefer high-quality images.
          #|      #|- **Attached images**: Keep them as-is and do not crop them; let the layout adapt to the image.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-layout" => (
          #|      #|# Typst Layout Guidelines
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Compile with the MoonBit Typst CLI
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run the CLI directly with `moonx cli/typst`:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```sh
          #|      #|moonx cli/typst compile slide.typ slide.pdf
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run this from the directory containing `slide.typ`. `moonx` resolves and runs
          #|      #|the CLI; there is no need to search SDK caches or create a MoonBit project.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Paragraph Justification
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Enable paragraph justification unless the user explicitly declines it:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#set par(justify: true)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Page Numbering
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When page numbers are needed, use Typst's automatic page numbering instead of hardcoding them. For example, to display the current page and total page count in a "1 / 3" style at the bottom right:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#set page(numbering: "1 / 1", number-align: right + bottom)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Finding Local Fonts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When you need to set a specific font in a Typst document, you can list the fonts available on the device in a way like the following (using Simplified Chinese SC as an example):
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|@shell.Pipeline([@shell.Cmd("moonx", ["cli/typst", "fonts"]), @shell.Cmd("rg", ["SC"])]).output()
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-syntax-cautions" => (
          #|      #|# Typst Syntax Cautions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 0 - Fetch the official reference instead of guessing
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When unsure about any Typst detail (element syntax, function arguments, symbol names), do not guess: fetch the official documentation and treat it as the source of truth.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Markup / model elements: https://typst.app/docs/reference/model/
          #|      #|- Math: https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/
          #|      #|- Symbol table: https://typst.app/docs/reference/symbols/sym/
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 1 - Do not treat operator characters as literal text
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`/` is the fraction operator, so `1/2` produces a stacked fraction, equivalent to `frac(1, 2)`. To display a literal slash as a math symbol, use `\/`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$1/2$        // stacked fraction
          #|      #|$frac(1, 2)$ // same as above (stacked)
          #|      #|$1 \/ 2$     // a literal slash 1/2
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 2 - Do not invent directive or keyword syntax
          #|      #|
          #|      #|[`limits`](https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/attach/#functions-limits) is a function, not a modifier token. Call it on the base to place its bottom and top attachments below and above the base instead of beside it.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$limits(A)_1^2 != A_1^2$
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 3 - Understand repeated alignment markers
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Each `&` marks one alignment point. Multiple alignment points create alternating right- and left-aligned columns. `&&` is valid: it represents two consecutive alignment points and has the same alignment behavior as `& &`, as documented in the [official alignment reference](https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/#alignment).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Example: the first `&` aligns the equations at `=`, and the following `&&` makes the annotation column left-aligned.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$
          #|      #|  a &= b && "first equation" \
          #|      #|  c &= d && "second equation"
          #|      #|$
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 4 - Do not use TeX macros for symbols or functions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Typst symbol and function names never begin with a backslash (`\`). Do not write TeX macros such as `\dots` or `\cdots`. Use `dots` for a baseline ellipsis (`…`) and `dots.c` for a centered ellipsis (`⋯`).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$dots$   // … (baseline ellipsis)
          #|      #|$dots.c$ // ⋯ (centered ellipsis)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For other symbols you are unsure about, validate a code snippet with a command such as `moonx cli/typst eval '$dots$'`, or consult the [official symbol table](https://typst.app/docs/reference/symbols/sym/).
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-touying" => (
          #|      #|# Touying for PDF Animations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use Touying only when the user needs PDF frame animations, such as progressive reveals with `#pause`, parallel reveal sequences with `#meanwhile`, or stepwise math equation animations. Use plain Typst for static slides.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#import "@preview/touying:0.7.4": *
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|PDF animations work by advancing through multiple pages (subslides) representing successive states of a logical slide. See the official [Dynamic Slides guide](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/category/dynamic-slides).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Consult the [official tutorials](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/category/tutorials) when implementing Touying features. Follow their setup and examples, check compatibility with the pinned version, and verify unfamiliar APIs rather than guessing syntax.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- [Simple animations](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/tutorials/dynamic/simple): `#pause` and `#meanwhile`.
          #|      #|- [Math equation animations](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/tutorials/dynamic/equation): reveal equations and derivations step by step.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/writing" => (
          #|      #|# Writing Style for Presentations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## General Principles
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Lead with the point**: Open with the main conclusion or question. Make titles convey specific information rather than generic section labels.
          #|      #|- **Be specific**: Name the subject, action, and result. Replace vague language such as "empower," "leverage," and "synergy" with facts and details. Avoid hype.
          #|      #|- **Write concisely**: Keep each sentence focused on one idea. Remove repetition, empty modifiers, and unnecessary setup while preserving conditions and explanations needed for understanding.
          #|      #|- **Make the reasoning clear**: Support claims with evidence and explain causes, tradeoffs, and scope. Distinguish facts, goals, and assumptions; do not present speculation as an established conclusion.
          #|      #|- **Use consistent terminology**: Explain concepts and abbreviations according to the audience's knowledge. Use the same name for the same concept throughout.
          #|      #|- **Match detail to purpose**: Make text for spoken presentations easy to scan; preserve complete arguments in materials intended for reading. Avoid rigid word limits.
          #|      #|- **Make recommendations actionable**: State the action, expected outcome, and necessary conditions. For collaborative work, specify owners and deadlines. Avoid closing with slogans.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Management Reports
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Use professional language appropriate to the presenter's role. Titles should state performance, causes, or requests so readers can follow the report by scanning its titles alone.
          #|      #|- Example title: "Q3 gross margin fell 3.2 percentage points, mainly due to higher raw material costs," rather than "Business Performance Report."
          #|      #|- Present results alongside their comparison baselines. Do not exaggerate achievements or omit unfavorable facts.
          #|      #|- Give actions a priority, owner, and deadline. Explain the expected impact and alternatives when requesting resources.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Technical and Engineering Presentations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- State conclusions directly. Avoid suspense or saving the answer for a final reveal.
          #|      #|- Follow the decision sequence: goals and constraints → current state and bottlenecks → mechanisms and evidence → tradeoffs → rollout and rollback → risks and next steps.
          #|      #|- Use text to explain motivations, tradeoffs, and evidence; use diagrams to show call relationships and data flow. Avoid repeating the same information in both.
          #|      #|- Ground broad descriptions such as "high availability," "high concurrency," and "cloud-native" in specific SLAs, QPS figures, protocols, or components. Avoid marketing language such as "revolutionary," "redefining," and "disruptive."
          #|      #|- For performance claims, state the environment, versions, sample size, and load conditions. Use appropriate percentiles or confidence intervals rather than reporting only averages. Distinguish the current state, targets, assumptions, and verified conclusions.
          #|      #|- Close with a path to adoption or implementation, rather than only "Thank you" or an unsupported slogan.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Analysis and Decision Support
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Work from conclusions toward evidence: state the judgment in the title, then develop supporting claims that each stand on their own and have verifiable evidence.
          #|      #|- Explain chart findings, the claims they support, their implications for decisions, and the data definitions and limitations.
          #|      #|- Preserve depth in materials intended for reading. Make conclusions easy to scan and evidence available for close reading; do not impose the minimal text requirements of a launch presentation.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Business Proposals and Launches
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Persuasive proposals**: Develop the argument through pain points → solution and advantages → value. Support each advantage with data, a case study, or a comparison. Close with the action you want the audience to take.
          #|      #|- **Launch presentations**: Present one idea per slide, aiming for a single line of body text. Favor images and short sentences. A possible narrative is interest → conflict → solution reveal → significance.
          #|      #|- Choose the approach for the audience and purpose. Avoid applying launch-style suspense and minimal copy to proposals that require substantial evidence.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Education and Training
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Use titles to pose learning questions or state conclusions. Give learners definitions or conclusions they can restate. When prompting thought, pose the question before developing the answer.
          #|      #|- Express learning objectives as observable actions, such as "explain the cause," "compare approaches," or "complete the procedure," rather than simply "understand," "master," or "be familiar with."
          #|      #|- Define new concepts before explaining their boundaries, components, or counterexamples. Do not introduce terms only in figure captions.
          #|      #|- Show the reasoning process, key tradeoffs, and common mistakes, not just the final answer.
          #|      #|- Motivate learning with real situations and connect it to practice through tasks, checklists, or exercises. Section summaries should answer the section's questions rather than repeat every slide title.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    _ => fail("agent-guide: unknown guide ID: " + id)
          #|  }
          #|}
          #|
          #|fn main {
          #|  let response : Json = match @env.args() {
          #|    [_] => {"version":1,"guides":[{"id":"artifacts","description":"Creating, packaging, and presenting session artifacts, including interactive answers."},{"id":"desktop-proton","description":"Building native MoonBit desktop applications with Proton."},{"id":"desktop-proton/examples","description":"Small Proton examples for native desktop applications."},{"id":"diagrams","description":"Choosing between prose, tables, charts, and diagrams, and selecting a diagram format."},{"id":"diagrams/charts","description":"Quantitative charts for trends, comparisons, distributions, and correlations."},{"id":"diagrams/diago","description":"Architecture and dependency diagrams with Diago or D2."},{"id":"diagrams/katex","description":"Explanatory diagrams drawn with KaTeX boxes and arrows."},{"id":"diagrams/mermaid","description":"Flowcharts and state diagrams with Mermaid."},{"id":"diagrams/plantuml","description":"Sequence and class diagrams with PlantUML."},{"id":"frontend-rabbita","description":"Building browser UIs and full-stack MoonBit applications with Rabbita."},{"id":"git-shipping","description":"Git authorship, commits, pull requests, and CI investigations."},{"id":"markdown-replies","description":"Markdown conventions for all user-facing replies."},{"id":"mcp","description":"Discovering and using external services through MCP."},{"id":"moonbit-development","description":"Developing, refactoring, testing, and looking up APIs in MoonBit projects."},{"id":"moongrep","description":"Structural code search and linting with moongrep."},{"id":"presentation","description":"Presenting local files, previewing deliverables, citing sources, and offering interactive answers."},{"id":"slides/design","description":"Visual design for presentation slides."},{"id":"slides/typst-layout","description":"Slide layout in Typst."},{"id":"slides/typst-syntax-cautions","description":"Common Typst syntax mistakes in slide source."},{"id":"slides/typst-touying","description":"PDF slide animations and progressive reveals with Touying."},{"id":"slides/writing","description":"Writing and editing slide text."}]}
          #|    [_, id] => { "version": 1, "id": id, "format": "markdown", "content": content(id) }
          #|    _ => fail("agent-guide: expected zero or one guide ID")
          #|  }
          #|  println(response.stringify())
          #|}
          #|
        ),
      )
